package instar:text@0.1.0;

// Instar's text capability, as a package of its own rather than more surface
// on `instar:kernel`.
//
// The kernel world is guest *lifecycle*: events, operations, cancellation, and
// the UI commit. Text is an optional host service a guest may or may not use,
// and folding it into the kernel would start a list — text-buffer, text-view,
// image, audio-graph, gpu-surface, file-handle — that ends with one giant
// runtime API instead of a kernel plus capabilities.
//
// `instar-kernel` assembles the world and generates the bindings; it does not
// semantically own this interface. `instar-text`, the Rust crate that
// implements the text model, does not know this file exists: it has no
// Wasmtime, no WIT, and no idea a guest is involved. Putting `text.wit` there
// would gradually turn "the text model" into "the guest ABI adapter for text".

interface text-types {
    /// One replacement of a byte range, as the wire carries it.
    ///
    /// UTF-8 byte offsets, because that is what the storage edits in and what
    /// the revision protocol carries -- see PHASE-3 "Coordinates". A consumer
    /// wanting row/column derives it from its own text state.
    record text-edit {
        range-start: u64,
        range-end: u64,
        replacement: string,
    }

    /// An edit the host applied, and what it moved the replica between.
    record applied-edit {
        base-revision: u64,
        resulting-revision: u64,
        edit: text-edit,
    }

    /// What the host owes a guest about one buffer.
    variant edit-notification {
        /// Edits to apply, oldest first.
        edits(list<applied-edit>),
        /// History was discarded and the guest must re-read. Carries the
        /// revision the replica has reached. Reporting this does **not** clear
        /// it: only an authoritative read re-arms, so an edit landing between
        /// hearing it and reading cannot be lost.
        desynchronized(u64),
    }

    /// Why a text resource could not be created.
    ///
    /// Deliberately not a generic error string: each variant is a bound the
    /// host enforces, and a guest that hits one should be able to tell which.
    variant text-error {
        /// The live-buffer ceiling was reached. A bound on *live* resources,
        /// not on historical ones -- slots are reused.
        too-many-buffers(u32),
        /// The live-view ceiling was reached.
        too-many-views(u32),
        /// The handle named a resource this generation cannot reach: it was
        /// dropped, or it belonged to a generation that no longer exists.
        no-such-resource,
        /// The host could not answer -- it is shutting down, or the thread
        /// that owns the text subsystem is gone.
        unavailable,
        /// A live `next-edit` is already asleep on this buffer. At most one
        /// outstanding call per guest per buffer; a second is refused rather
        /// than queued behind the first, because there is nothing left for it
        /// to read and two sleepers would race to serve the same edits.
        already-waiting,
        /// An `apply-edits` batch exceeded the inbound count or byte bound,
        /// checked before any edit in it is attempted.
        edit-batch-too-large,
        /// One coarse refusal for every way a submitted byte range is
        /// unusable: inverted, out of bounds, or off a character boundary.
        /// Deliberately not three cases -- the storage-layer distinction is
        /// `instar-text`'s to know, not this interface's to expose.
        invalid-edit,
        /// The document that would result from an otherwise-valid batch
        /// exceeds the host's size ceiling.
        buffer-too-large,
    }

    /// What `apply-edits` resolved to, once the request cleared every
    /// `text-error`.
    ///
    /// Not folded into `text-error`: a guest that lost an optimistic race
    /// submitted a well-formed request against text that moved underneath
    /// it. That is an ordinary outcome to handle, not a malformed one.
    variant apply-edits-result {
        /// The batch applied. The buffer's new revision.
        applied(u64),
        /// `expected-revision` did not match. The buffer's current revision;
        /// nothing in the batch was applied.
        conflict(u64),
    }

    /// The exact bytes of a byte range, and the revision they were read at.
    ///
    /// `contents` is guaranteed complete, valid UTF-8: the same boundary
    /// check `apply-edits` uses refuses a range that lands mid-character, so
    /// a successful read never has to describe a partial code point.
    record range-contents {
        contents: string,
        revision: u64,
    }

    /// The whole document, and the revision this generation is now re-armed
    /// at.
    ///
    /// Structurally identical to `range-contents`, kept as its own type
    /// anyway: a `range-contents` value carries no promise about
    /// synchronization state, and a `snapshot` always does -- receiving one
    /// means the relationship for this buffer was just re-armed at exactly
    /// `revision`. Conflating the two would invite a reader to assume a
    /// `read-range` result re-arms something, which is precisely the
    /// assumption `read-range`'s own doc refuses.
    record snapshot {
        contents: string,
        revision: u64,
    }
}

/// Host-owned text resources, leased to a guest as capabilities.
///
/// # These are leases, not documents
///
/// A `text-buffer` handle is a capability naming a buffer the *host* owns on
/// the thread that owns presentation. The rope, its revisions and its journal
/// are never in the guest's resource table, and never in its linear memory.
/// Dropping a handle ends the guest's ability to name a document; whether the
/// document itself ends is the host's decision, which is what makes guest
/// restart survivable later.
///
/// # Synchronous here, async in the host -- except the one that must suspend
///
/// `create-empty-buffer` and `create-view` are not WIT `async func`s.
/// Declaring one would opt into Component Model async semantics -- overlapping
/// subtasks and a different ABI -- when what is actually wanted is the
/// opposite: the guest suspends inside the call while the host's Rust future
/// awaits the thread that owns the text subsystem. `bindgen!`'s
/// `imports: { default: async | trappable }` is that distinction, and it
/// needs nothing from the WIT.
///
/// `next-edit` breaks that pattern on purpose (C2). It genuinely is a WIT
/// `async func`, because the alternative -- a guest that cannot await an edit
/// and a UI event at the same time -- is worse than the overlapping-subtask
/// machinery this package otherwise avoids. See its own doc comment for why.
interface text {
    use text-types.{
        text-error, edit-notification, text-edit, apply-edits-result, range-contents, snapshot,
    };

    resource text-buffer;
    resource text-view;

    /// Opens an empty document.
    ///
    /// Predates `create-buffer` below: Package B could not yet raise canonical
    /// transfer, copy limits, and bootstrap revisions all at once, so this
    /// stayed the only constructor until C4a. Kept now because "no contents to
    /// transfer" is a real, common case that a `create-buffer("")` call would
    /// only reach by more roundabout means.
    create-empty-buffer: func() -> result<text-buffer, text-error>;

    /// Opens a document pre-populated with `contents`.
    ///
    /// Born `Synchronized` at revision 0, exactly as `create-empty-buffer`'s
    /// document is. Bootstrap establishes a baseline, not an edit: the guest
    /// supplied these bytes itself, so this generates no `edit-notification`
    /// and no undo entry -- reporting them back would be the host telling the
    /// guest its own text as news, and an undo entry would make this document's
    /// first state undoable into an empty one it never had.
    ///
    /// `contents` past `instar-text`'s size ceiling is refused with
    /// `buffer-too-large`. That refusal means the payload *reached* Instar and
    /// was still too large -- Wasmtime's own hostcall-fuel budget is what
    /// stops a payload too large to lift at all, before this function's host
    /// implementation ever runs. See `resource::TEXT_TRANSFER_HOSTCALL_FUEL`
    /// in `instar-kernel`.
    create-buffer: func(contents: string) -> result<text-buffer, text-error>;

    /// Opens a view onto a buffer.
    ///
    /// Borrowed, because a view does not take ownership of the document: many
    /// views may name one buffer, and the buffer outlives any of them.
    create-view: func(buffer: borrow<text-buffer>) -> result<text-view, text-error>;

    /// Suspends until the host has something to say about this buffer.
    ///
    /// **The one `async func` in this interface, and it has to be.** Marking a
    /// plain import async in `bindgen!` makes only the *host implementation*
    /// async -- to WebAssembly the call still blocks, so the guest task is
    /// parked and can do nothing else. A guest must be able to await an edit
    /// and a UI event at once, which is only expressible when the WIT itself
    /// says `async`.
    ///
    /// The same line the kernel world already draws: `next-event`, `commit`
    /// and `await-op` are async because they suspend on an external event,
    /// while `start`, `cancel` and the `create-*` pair are plain because they
    /// are bounded.
    ///
    /// At most one outstanding call per guest per buffer. A second is refused
    /// rather than queued behind the first: there is nothing for it to read,
    /// and two sleepers would race to serve the same edits.
    next-edit: async func(buffer: borrow<text-buffer>)
        -> result<edit-notification, text-error>;

    /// Applies a batch of edits optimistically against the revision this
    /// guest last saw.
    ///
    /// Plain `func`, not `async`: unlike `next-edit`, this call is bounded by
    /// how long the thread that owns the text subsystem takes to answer, the
    /// same shape `create-view` already has.
    ///
    /// `expected-revision` is checked against the buffer's current revision
    /// before any edit in `edits` is even looked at -- a stale revision is a
    /// `conflict`, not a reason to validate a batch authored against text
    /// that has already moved. The batch itself is atomic: on any refusal,
    /// nothing in it is applied, exactly as a refused UI commit promotes
    /// nothing.
    ///
    /// A batch this generation submits is never echoed back to it through
    /// its own `next-edit` -- it already knows what it applied. Every other
    /// generation watching this buffer hears about it normally.
    apply-edits: func(
        buffer: borrow<text-buffer>,
        expected-revision: u64,
        edits: list<text-edit>,
    ) -> result<apply-edits-result, text-error>;

    /// Reads an exact byte range, and the revision it was read at.
    ///
    /// **Strictly observational.** This call never advances or re-arms this
    /// generation's synchronization state for `buffer` -- it is not the
    /// recovery mechanism, and treating it as one is the double-application
    /// hazard C4's design explicitly refuses to ship:
    ///
    /// ```text
    /// guest apply-edits(expected=5) -> conflict(7)
    /// guest read-range(...)         -> bytes at revision 7
    /// queue still holds the two edits between 5 and 7
    /// ```
    ///
    /// A guest that adopted revision 7 from that read and then drained
    /// `next-edit` would apply both edits a second time. After a conflict, a
    /// guest either drains `next-edit` until caught up, or calls
    /// `resynchronize` -- the one call that atomically snapshots *and*
    /// re-arms, in that order, as one owner-thread transition.
    ///
    /// A malformed range -- inverted, out of bounds, or off a character
    /// boundary -- is `invalid-edit`, the same coarse refusal `apply-edits`
    /// uses for the same underlying check.
    read-range: func(
        buffer: borrow<text-buffer>,
        start: u64,
        end: u64,
    ) -> result<range-contents, text-error>;

    /// Atomically snapshots the whole document and re-arms this generation's
    /// relationship at exactly that revision, discarding whatever it owed
    /// before.
    ///
    /// The one call in this interface that both reads *and* changes
    /// synchronization state, and the only legal way to change it other than
    /// draining `next-edit` one batch at a time. Legal whether this
    /// generation is currently synchronized or desynchronized -- a guest
    /// that would rather not replay a backlog it has just read past may
    /// discard it the same way a genuinely desynchronized one recovers,
    /// which is also what keeps this path exercised by ordinary use instead
    /// of only by faults.
    ///
    /// The snapshot and the re-arm are not two calls a guest makes in
    /// sequence; they are one atomic transition on the thread that owns the
    /// text subsystem. Nothing can observe or mutate this relationship
    /// between the two, so the revision reported in `snapshot` and the
    /// revision this generation is re-armed at are always the same value --
    /// see `instar-host`'s `TextHost::resynchronize`, which is the authority
    /// on why that is a property of the code, not merely of testing it.
    resynchronize: func(buffer: borrow<text-buffer>) -> result<snapshot, text-error>;
}
